--- body ---
## Context

The current `inspect()` path verifies native pack identity and versions but
never checks its text content. A well-formed empty pack therefore follows the
same `current` path as a pack containing text. Ticket 0744 consumes the
corrected classification; SPEC.md §5.2.7 owns its meaning and display.

The earlier external pdftotext sample concerned only initial pages and was
not a complete native-pack content inspection. It motivates investigation,
but does not prove a document has no text layer, diagnose a scan, or justify
extrapolating an empty-pack population. No new measurement was made in the
design correction. Prior reported figures are not acceptance evidence.

## Actions

- Inspect the native format and reader for an authoritative complete-content
  indicator. If unavailable, use bounded streaming inspection of all relevant
  text content, with yielding/cancellation; do not read whole packs into RAM.
- Define and document which native text fields establish presence, and test
  complete-content semantics. Empty means verified absence of text, not a
  sparse-content threshold. A partial sample or read failure is unknown.
- Introduce the verified-empty classification outside indexed coverage, with
  a non-repeating scheduling treatment. Preserve freshness/identity checks:
  source or processor changes must invalidate an earlier empty observation.
- Propagate the classification through the cache-hit path, coverage counters,
  census snapshots and 0744's view. A generic cache hit must not restore
  `current` for a previously verified empty pack.
- Commit the probe and evidence in verification/ before claiming the reader
  mechanism has been established. Reference them here. No source-layer or OCR
  diagnosis follows from empty native content alone.

## Reader-contract evidence

`verification/empty-pack-0760/` — `probe.mjs` run against Zotero's own shipped
reader (sha256 `340f44fe…`) and the author's 46 packs, `reading.json` the
output, `reader-contract.md` the finding. In short: the format carries NO
positive text indicator, so absence can only be enumerated; what makes an
enumeration trustworthy is the reader's refusal to open a pack whose declared
content extent misses the file's own length, demonstrated by four corruptions of
a real pack that each throw; and the one completeness indicator that does exist
is negative, the catalogue's per-page `extractionDegraded` stamp.

## Verification

- [x] A well-formed empty native pack differs from indexed text.
- [x] Sparse nonempty text and text appearing only late in a document remain
      nonempty; partial reads and corrupt/unreadable content are not empty.
      Whitespace-only runs, text on a block's own property, text two levels down
      inside `listitem` children, an invalid block count, a range that is not an
      array, a chunk that throws, and a reader without the block API at all.
- [x] A cache hit preserves the verified-empty result; source and processor
      changes invalidate it and permit normal reinspection/re-extraction.
- [x] Empty packs do not create a repeated extraction loop. Asserted across
      three sweeps in one session, not only the first.
- [x] Coverage counts and 0744's data view agree after incremental updates
      and completed reconciliation. They come from `state.counts` and from
      `censusSnapshot.members`, two places that agree only because `publish()`
      writes them together; that is now asserted rather than assumed.
- [x] Content inspection remains bounded, yielding and cancellable. Bounded and
      yielding were already true; cancellable was not — the block walk consulted
      nothing and ran on after teardown. It now stops on `alive`.
- [x] Reader-contract evidence is committed and referenced here (above).
- [x] `make check` passes.

## Correction made while verifying

The empty verdict was being given on enumeration alone. Zotero's worker marks a
page `extractionDegraded` when it has to fall back, and
`verification/SDT-PALGRAVE-AUDIT.md` had already recorded that such a pack is no
evidence that every page was fully analysed — but `inspect()` never read the
catalogue. A textless degraded pack would therefore have been reported as "No
extracted text", a confident claim SPEC.md §5.2.7 forbids. Measured on the
author's library: 29 of 11 909 pages are degraded, all inside one text-bearing
pack, and no pack is both textless and degraded, so the defect was latent rather
than observed. Fixed anyway, since the combination is reachable by construction.

## Exit criteria

Verified empty native content is distinguished from searchable text throughout
inspection, caching, scheduling and reporting under SPEC.md §5.2.7. No native
extraction settings, source files or extraction implementation are changed.
